---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Accessibility"
  description="MathemAct's accessibility statement: what we aim for, what is known not to work, and how to report a problem."
  lead="This site aims to meet WCAG 2.1 Level AA. Where it falls short, say so."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <h2>What we aim for</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct is built to the{' '}
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/">Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2.1</a>{' '}
    Level AA. The relevant targets:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>All form controls have labels bound to their inputs.</li>
    <li>Interactive elements have visible keyboard focus indicators.</li>
    <li>
      Colour is never the sole carrier of meaning — outcome states (worked, partially
      worked, did not work) use both colour and a label.
    </li>
    <li>
      The site respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>. There are no animations
      by default.
    </li>
    <li>Page structure uses semantic landmarks: <code>header</code>, <code>main</code>,
      <code>footer</code>, and <code>nav</code>.</li>
    <li>The reading order in the HTML matches the visual order.</li>
    <li>Mathematical content is rendered with KaTeX, which produces accessible MathML.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Type and legibility</h2>

  <p>
    WCAG places no requirement on typeface choice, so nothing in this section is a
    conformance claim. It is here because the type is the first thing you meet on a page,
    and the choices behind it are checkable.
  </p>

  <p>
    The site is set in <a href="https://www.ibm.com/plex/">IBM Plex</a> — Serif for
    headings, Sans for body text, Mono for anything a machine could parse, such as model
    names, versions, dates and scores. It is an open-licence family designed for interface
    text, and it earns its place here rather than being decorative:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The characters that get confused are drawn apart.</strong> Capital
      <code>I</code> carries top and bottom crossbars, lowercase <code>l</code> a tail to
      the right, and the digit <code>1</code> a flag and a full foot — so
      <code>Il1</code> reads as three different characters in all three families. In Mono,
      the zero is dotted. That matters most exactly where it appears: a version string is
      unambiguous at 13px.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The x-height is 74% of the cap height</strong>, which is generous, and is
      what keeps the smallest metadata readable rather than merely small.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Body text is 17px at a line height of 1.6</strong>, and long prose 1.7. Every
      size is set in <code>rem</code> and nothing is pinned to a pixel, so browser zoom
      and a larger default text size set in the operating system both work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accented characters come from the same faces as the rest of the text.</strong>
      Erdős, Ważewski and Lindelöf are set in Plex throughout, and do not fall back to a
      different typeface partway through a name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing depends on a font arriving.</strong> The files are self-hosted and
      declared <code>font-display: swap</code>, so text is readable in a system face
      immediately; each family has a full fallback stack behind it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No text is delivered as an image.</strong> The one diagram, on the home page,
      is marked <code>role="img"</code> and announces a title and a description.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      In Plex Sans and Plex Serif, <code>0</code> and <code>O</code> differ only in width.
      Only Mono dots its zero, which is why anything meant to be read character by
      character is set in Mono — but a stray identifier in ordinary prose is ambiguous.
    </li>
    <li>
      The handwritten labels on the home page diagram are set in Kalam, which distinguishes
      almost nothing: <code>I</code>, <code>l</code> and <code>1</code> are near-identical
      strokes. It is confined to that one drawing and appears in no prose anywhere on the
      site, and the diagram's description carries the same content in text.
    </li>
    <li>
      The moderation screen — only visible to logged-in moderators — has not been tested
      with a screen reader.
    </li>
    <li>
      Complex TeX expressions in reports may not be fully navigable with older screen
      readers that do not support MathML.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reporting a problem</h2>

  <p>
    If you find something that stops you using this site, write to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. Describe what you were
    trying to do, what happened, and what browser and assistive technology you were using.
    This is a personal project with no dedicated accessibility resource, but reports are
    taken seriously and fixes are prioritised over new features.
  </p>

  <h2>Enforcement</h2>

  <p>
    This statement covers the MathemAct website at{' '}
    <a href="https://emmazanoli.github.io/MathemAct/">emmazanoli.github.io/MathemAct</a>.
    MathemAct is an independent personal project and is not subject to the EU Web
    Accessibility Directive, which applies to public sector bodies. This statement is
    published voluntarily.
  </p>
</Page>
